---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Site
  title="Set up a device"
  description="Install LightVM firmware for your exact board, connect it to the browser and check that it is ready for your lighting project."
>
  <section class="wrap page-intro narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">FROM BOARD TO SOMETHING YOU MADE</p>
    <h1>Set up a device</h1>
    <p class="intro">Start with the exact model in your hand.</p>
    <p>
      Firmware is the software installed on the microcontroller. LightVM
      firmware lets it run your lighting programs and communicate with the
      browser. The workspace shows published firmware for your exact board, its
      features and what installation will change.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button dark" href={`${base}studio/#/firmware`}>
        Choose firmware →
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${base}studio/#/devices`}>
        Connect an existing device →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose">
    <h2>Still gathering the parts?</h2>
    <p>
      Start with the <a href={`${base}build/`}>build-your-own guide</a> for a
      board, LEDs, wiring and power. Installation needs firmware for your exact
      model. If the catalogue has no approved build, there is no public browser
      installation for that board yet; the guide also links to the developer
      build instructions.
    </p>
    <h2>For a new or unbootable board</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Read the physical model printed on the board. Pico and Pico W share a
        bootloader, so USB alone cannot distinguish them.
      </li>
      <li>
        Choose that model and a published build. ESP boards get a chip, flash
        and identity check over USB. Pico boards use a verified UF2 download for
        the selected model.
      </li>
      <li>
        Save anything you need before installing. Installation erases saved
        programs, settings and nicknames.
      </li>
      <li>
        Reconnect after installation. The workspace compares the device identity
        and running build with your selection.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <h2>For an existing device</h2>
    <p>
      Connect in <strong>My devices</strong> first to read its identity,
      nickname, software version and installed features. The firmware view shows
      which features an installation would add or remove, and whether you have
      selected an older version.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once LightVM is installed, you can send new animations to the board from
      the lighting editor. Changing a pattern does not require reinstalling the
      firmware. Follow the{" "}
      <a href={`${base}build/#first-pattern`}>first-colour steps</a> to try it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A settings export contains the nickname and available configuration. It
      does not recover editable program source or every stored object. Keep your
      source files separately.
    </p>
    <h2>Browser and connection support</h2>
    <p>
      Use desktop Chrome or Edge for a USB connection to the board. Pico
      installation uses a UF2 file you download and copy to the board’s USB
      drive; downloading that file works in other browsers too. When connecting
      over your local network, your browser may ask for permission.
    </p>
    <p>
      Firmware installation currently uses USB. A download or installation
      button appears only when that method is approved for the selected build.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href={`${base}hardware/`}>
      Check the board guide →
    </a>
  </section>
</Site>
